From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sqz kernel recipe <sqz-kernel@squeezefs>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 07:40:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 29/29] sqz: fuse-uring zc payload retention

sqz: fuse-uring zc payload retention

Approach B's ACK-early accelerator (design-device-overlay §8 / design
kernel-zc-write-v2 §3): the daemon may COMMIT (ACK the write to the
application) while the sparse-slot pages remain registered, DMA from
those bvecs later, and RELEASE the slot explicitly when done.

Depends on 0025: after fuse_request_end() the folios' owners drop their
references; only the imu-held references from 0025 keep a retained
slot's pages live.

uapi (include/uapi/linux/fuse.h; struct size stays 24 bytes, union
absorbs the new member; BUILD_BUG_ON in dev_uring.c):

  FUSE_IO_URING_CMD_RELEASE_PAYLOAD = 3
  FUSE_URING_PAYLOAD_RETENTION      (1 << 2)   /* init.flags */
  FUSE_URING_COMMIT_RETAIN          (1 << 0)   /* commit.flags */

Arming: PAYLOAD_RETENTION is accepted only with ZERO_COPY; refuse
-EINVAL otherwise. Re-REGISTER consistency includes the retention bit.

COMMIT with RETAIN is valid only when the queue is retention-armed AND
the ent is zero_copied AND the registration was ITER_SOURCE (a write).
Any violation fails the commit with -EINVAL without consuming the
request. Read-direction retention is refused by design.

On a valid RETAIN the request ends normally (write(2) returns) but
io_buffer_unregister() is skipped and the ent goes FRRS_RETAINED. The
COMMIT_AND_FETCH cmd stays pending (no CQE) — re-arm is deferred
exactly like a §5.4 payload lease. Pin accounting is structural: one
retained slot = one ent out of the fetch pool, bounded by
zero_copy_depth.

RELEASE_PAYLOAD looks up by commit_id:
  - FRRS_RETAINED: unregister, re-enter fetch, RELEASE completes 0
  - live un-retained commit: -EBUSY
  - unknown / already released: -ENOENT
  - unknown opcode (old kernel): -EINVAL / -EOPNOTSUPP
The errno split is the negotiation probe.

Teardown drains FRRS_RETAINED like userspace-held ents whose request
already ended — no stop_fuse_req_end, parked cmd completed through the
existing IO_URING_F_CANCEL-safe path (-ENOTCONN). Pages survive until
the ring releases them (0025's law). A ratelimited pr_warn fires when
teardown sees a nonzero retained population (daemon lost RELEASEs).
No kernel timer: a leaked retention is bounded, visible as fetch
starvation, and reclaimed at teardown.

Stability law (kernel documents; daemon enforces): after
COMMIT-with-RETAIN the application legally owns its buffer again.
Bytes that land are sampled at DMA time — unstable-write semantics
(NFS UNSTABLE/COMMIT class). Page-cache writeback is sound by
composition (redirty is a new WRITE; overlay newest-wins). O_DIRECT
GUP pages are NOT sound in general; ACK-early those only under an
explicit opt-in. A read intersecting an ACKed-but-incomplete store
must wait for that store or serve from its retained source; it may
never treat the range as an uncovered old-map gap.

A retention-armed queue with zero RETAIN commits behaves bit-identically
to a zc queue.

[sqz 6.19.14] io_uring_sqe_cmd; cancel moves FRRS_RETAINED onto ent_in_userspace like AVAILABLE (no kfree — teardown owns the ent); prepare_send is the simpler 6.19 error arm.

---
diff --git a/Documentation/filesystems/fuse/fuse-io-uring.rst b/Documentation/filesystems/fuse/fuse-io-uring.rst
index 11c244b63..f9483f28c 100644
--- a/Documentation/filesystems/fuse/fuse-io-uring.rst
+++ b/Documentation/filesystems/fuse/fuse-io-uring.rst
@@ -154,3 +154,39 @@ Please note that the server is not able to directly access these pages, it
 must go through the io-uring interface to read/write to them. The pages are
 unregistered once the server replies to the request. Non-zero-copyable
 payload (if needed) is placed in a buffer from the kernel-managed buffer ring.
+
+Payload retention
+=================
+
+Zero-copy write payloads can outlive the FUSE request. A privileged server
+that set ``FUSE_URING_PAYLOAD_RETENTION`` (requires ``FUSE_URING_ZERO_COPY``)
+may commit a write with ``FUSE_URING_COMMIT_RETAIN``. The application's
+write(2) then returns while the sparse-slot pages stay registered. The
+COMMIT_AND_FETCH command stays pending (no CQE, no fetch) until the server
+issues ``FUSE_IO_URING_CMD_RELEASE_PAYLOAD`` with the same ``commit_id``.
+
+Read-direction retention is refused (``-EINVAL``): a retained READ dest has
+no consumer once the reply is committed. Illegal RETAIN (queue not armed,
+not zero-copied, or not ``ITER_SOURCE``) fails the commit with ``-EINVAL``
+without consuming the request. ``RELEASE_PAYLOAD`` on an unknown
+``commit_id`` returns ``-ENOENT``; on a live un-retained commit, ``-EBUSY``.
+Unknown opcode on an old kernel is ``-EINVAL``/``-EOPNOTSUPP`` — that split
+is the negotiation probe.
+
+After COMMIT-with-RETAIN, write(2) has returned: the application legally owns
+its buffer again and may modify it, while the retained DMA has not yet
+sampled the pages. The bytes that land are sampled at DMA time within the
+retention window — **unstable-write semantics** (the NFS UNSTABLE/COMMIT
+class). Kernel-side this is a documentation duty; the daemon decides which
+writes may ACK early.
+
+A read intersecting an ACKed-but-incomplete store must wait for that store
+or serve from its retained source; it may never treat the range as an
+uncovered old-map gap.
+
+Pin accounting is structural: one retained slot is one ent out of the fetch
+pool, bounded by ``zero_copy_depth``. The kernel does not carry a timer. A
+leaked retention is reclaimed at teardown (ratelimited ``pr_warn``). Page
+lifetime follows the io_uring registration (see the folio-get / imu-release
+contract); request lifetime follows FUSE. Neither trusts the other's
+ordering.
